  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">USAID - The Horticulture Advancement Activity (THAzA)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Pakistan):  The Horticulture Advancement Activity (THAzA) is a five-year, $16.45 million Public International Organization grant to the United Nations Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO). Working with the local governments, THAzA is implemented in the Afghan-Pakistan border region in Balochistan and Khyber Pakhtunkhwa (KP) provinces to strengthen commercial horticulture value chains to create sustainable livelihoods for farmers. . .FY 2021 funding will be used by THAZA for value-added activities in the horticulture sector. The activities will include establishment of cold storage facilities in KP and Balochistan, fruits and vegetables grading, cleaning and packing facilities in the two provinces, and refrigerated transportation facilities for the perishable products. From the 2021 funding, $200,000 contributes to Pakistan&apos;s $10 million gender earmark, as it will be used to improve gender equality in the activity area. For example, USAID will award female-specific matching grants for fruit and vegetable drying, vegetable production under greenhouse tunnels, and zero-energy cold storage for potatoes. These grants will be accompanied with capacity building exercises and market linkages for female beneficiaries.. .The border region is the poorest in Pakistan. The agro-ecological conditions, which are favorable for the production of fruits and vegetables, make USAID&apos;s work to promote commercial horticulture value chains the optimal approach to create economic opportunities and reduce poverty. As the activity works closely with the KP government, THAzA will advance the USG&apos;s objective to increase the writ of the government along the border region by building provincial government capacity and promoting private sector-led inclusive economic growth...... .. .</narrative>
  </description>
